§ `Victims of crime financial assistance: the victim's questions`

§§ `Who is a victim under the scheme (ss 3, 9, 13, 15, 17, 20)`

@export Am I a primary victim of an act of violence, able to apply for financial assistance under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 3, 9)? A primary victim is injured by a violent crime in Victoria committed against them, injured trying to arrest an offender, prevent the crime or help a victim, or a child injured by hearing, seeing or being exposed to it. You can apply in one capacity only for one act of violence (s 20).
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What the crime was, ignoring the offender's age, mental impairment or any defence: an offence punishable by imprisonment involving assault, injury or a threat of injury; a sexual offence (including rape); observation of the genital or anal region; stalking, child stealing or kidnapping; home invasion or aggravated home invasion; an offence the regulations prescribe; a conspiracy, incitement or attempt to commit one of these; or none of these
      `in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime happened in Victoria
      `injury or death resulted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime directly injured or killed someone (you or anyone else, wherever the injury happened)
      `how` IS A `How I was harmed` @desc How you were hurt: the crime was committed against you; trying to arrest someone you reasonably believed had committed it; trying to prevent it; trying to aid or rescue someone you reasonably believed was a victim; hearing, seeing or otherwise being exposed to its effects; or none of these
      `the harm` IS A `Kind of harm` @desc The harm you suffered: physical harm; psychological or psychiatric harm; a worsening of an injury you already had; trauma the scheme decision maker has determined to be an injury (on medical or psychological evidence that you need treatment or counselling); trauma not so determined; grief or distress only; harm arising only from loss of or damage to property; or no harm
      `direct result` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your harm was a direct result of the crime (or of trying to arrest, prevent or rescue)
      `under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the crime was committed
      `prescribed category` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a category of persons the regulations make primary victims; answer no if unsure
      `I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you committed the crime and are criminally responsible for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a primary victim` MEANS
    `s 9 -- I am a primary victim` `the offence` `in Victoria` `injury or death resulted` `how` `the harm` `direct result` `under 18` `prescribed category` `I committed it`

@export Am I a secondary victim of an act of violence, able to apply for financial assistance under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 13)? A secondary victim was present and injured by witnessing a violent crime in Victoria, or is the parent or guardian of a child victim and was injured by learning of it afterwards.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What the crime was, ignoring the offender's age, mental impairment or any defence: an offence punishable by imprisonment involving assault, injury or a threat of injury; a sexual offence (including rape); observation of the genital or anal region; stalking, child stealing or kidnapping; home invasion or aggravated home invasion; an offence the regulations prescribe; a conspiracy, incitement or attempt to commit one of these; or none of these
      `in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime happened in Victoria
      `injury or death resulted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime directly injured or killed someone
      `how I knew` IS A `How I came to know of it` @desc How you came to know of the crime: you were present at the scene and witnessed it; you became aware of it afterwards; or neither
      `the harm` IS A `Kind of harm` @desc The harm you suffered: physical harm; psychological or psychiatric harm; a worsening of an injury you already had; trauma the scheme decision maker has determined to be an injury; trauma not so determined; grief or distress only; harm arising only from loss of or damage to property; or no harm
      `direct result` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your harm was a direct result of witnessing, or learning of, the crime
      `parent or guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a parent or guardian of the person the crime was committed against (a guardian appointed by VCAT or the Secretary does not count)
      `the victim was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person was under 18 when the crime was committed
      `I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you committed the crime and are criminally responsible for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a secondary victim` MEANS
    `s 13 -- I am a secondary victim` `the offence` `in Victoria` `injury or death resulted` `how I knew` `the harm` `direct result` `parent or guardian` `the victim was under 18` `I committed it`

@export Am I a related victim of an act of violence, able to apply for financial assistance under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 3, 15)? A related victim is a close family member, dependant or intimate partner of a person who died as a direct result of a violent crime in Victoria.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What the crime was, ignoring the offender's age, mental impairment or any defence: an offence punishable by imprisonment involving assault, injury or a threat of injury; a sexual offence (including rape); observation of the genital or anal region; stalking, child stealing or kidnapping; home invasion or aggravated home invasion; an offence the regulations prescribe; a conspiracy, incitement or attempt to commit one of these; or none of these
      `in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime happened in Victoria
      `they died of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the crime was committed against died as a direct result of it
      `relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc What you were to the person who died: spouse or domestic partner; parent, guardian or step-parent; child or step-child; a child of whom they were the guardian; sibling or step-sibling; or another relationship
      `genuine relationship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a genuine personal relationship with them when they died
      `they regarded me as close family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they regarded you as a close family member when they died
      `decision maker considers it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker considers it reasonable to treat you as a close family member, given your relationship; answer no if not yet decided
      `dependant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were wholly or substantially dependent on their income when they died (or would have been, but for their incapacity from the injury that killed them), or are their child born after their death who would have been
      `intimate relationship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had an intimate personal relationship with them
      `I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you committed the crime and are criminally responsible for it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a related victim` MEANS
    `s 15 -- I am a related victim` `the offence` `in Victoria` `they died of it` `relationship` `genuine relationship` `they regarded me as close family` `decision maker considers it reasonable` `dependant` `intimate relationship` `I committed it`

@export I paid, or owe, for the funeral of a person killed by a violent crime: can I be paid assistance for the funeral expenses under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 17)? The payment covers the expenses actually and reasonably incurred, on top of any assistance you get as a secondary or related victim.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What the crime was, ignoring the offender's age, mental impairment or any defence: an offence punishable by imprisonment involving assault, injury or a threat of injury; a sexual offence (including rape); observation of the genital or anal region; stalking, child stealing or kidnapping; home invasion or aggravated home invasion; an offence the regulations prescribe; a conspiracy, incitement or attempt to commit one of these; or none of these
      `in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime happened in Victoria
      `they died of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person the crime was committed against died as a direct result of it
      `funeral expenses` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid, or become liable for, funeral expenses for that person
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be paid for the funeral` MEANS
    `s 17 -- I may be paid for the funeral expenses` `the offence` `in Victoria` `they died of it` `funeral expenses`

§§ `How much can be paid (ss 10, 11, 14, 16, 18, 19, 37, 72)`

@export How much financial assistance can I be paid as a primary victim under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 10, 18, 19, 37(3))? Up to $60 000 (indexed yearly), with loss of earnings up to $20 000 (indexed) over the first 2 years, less what you have received for the same losses and any interim assistance. Special financial assistance is extra and asked separately. The answer assumes the scheme decision maker accepts each amount as reasonable.
GIVEN `counselling` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of reasonable counselling expenses you have incurred or are reasonably likely to incur
      `medical` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of medical expenses (including dental, optometry, physiotherapy, psychiatric, psychological, hospital and ambulance) incurred, or reasonably likely, as a direct result of the crime
      `lost earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of earnings lost, or likely to be lost, through total or partial incapacity for work in the 2 years after the crime
      `clothing` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of loss of or damage to the clothing you were wearing at the time
      `safety` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of safety-related expenses (for example changing locks or moving) incurred or reasonably likely as a direct result
      `other recovery` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of other expenses to help you recover; counted only in exceptional circumstances
      `exceptional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker accepts there are exceptional circumstances for the other recovery expenses
      `maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum for a primary victim in force for this financial year under the indexation notice (s 72); enter 0 to use the Act's $60 000
      `earnings maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss of earnings maximum in force for this financial year under the indexation notice (s 72); enter 0 to use the Act's $20 000
      `damages received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of damages, compensation or other payments (but not insurance or superannuation) you have already received for the same losses, including any Transport (Compliance and Miscellaneous) Act 1983 s 12 assistance; 0 if none
      `insurance received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you have already received for the same losses; 0 if none
      `likely payments counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars you are likely to recover or receive for the same losses (damages, WorkSafe, TAC or other schemes, but not insurance or superannuation) that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `likely insurance counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you are likely to receive for the same losses that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `interim paid` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of interim assistance already paid to you on this application; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I be paid as a primary victim` MEANS
    `s 10 -- the assistance a primary victim can be paid`
        (`A primary victim's expenses and losses` WITH
            `counselling` IS `counselling`
            `medical expenses` IS `medical`
            `loss of earnings in the first 2 years` IS `lost earnings`
            `clothing lost or damaged` IS `clothing`
            `safety-related expenses` IS `safety`
            `other recovery expenses` IS `other recovery`)
        `exceptional`
        `maximum in force`
        `earnings maximum in force`
        (`What I have received or am likely to receive for the same losses` WITH
            `damages and compensation received, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `damages received`
            `insurance or superannuation payments received` IS `insurance received`
            `likely amounts the decision maker takes into account, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `likely payments counted`
            `likely insurance or superannuation payments the decision maker takes into account` IS `likely insurance counted`)
        `interim paid`

@export How much financial assistance can I be paid as a secondary victim under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 14, 18, 19, 37(3))? Up to $50 000 (indexed yearly) for counselling and medical expenses; lost earnings (up to $20 000, indexed) and other recovery expenses only in exceptional circumstances; less what you have received for the same losses and any interim assistance.
GIVEN `counselling` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of reasonable counselling expenses you have incurred or are reasonably likely to incur
      `medical` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of medical expenses incurred, or reasonably likely, as a direct result of witnessing or learning of the crime
      `lost earnings` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of earnings lost, or likely to be lost, through incapacity for work in the 2 years after the crime; counted only in exceptional circumstances
      `other recovery` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of other expenses to help you recover; counted only in exceptional circumstances
      `exceptional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker accepts there are exceptional circumstances
      `maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum for a secondary victim in force for this financial year under the indexation notice (s 72); enter 0 to use the Act's $50 000
      `earnings maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The loss of earnings maximum in force for this financial year under the indexation notice (s 72); enter 0 to use the Act's $20 000
      `damages received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of damages, compensation or other payments (but not insurance or superannuation) you have already received for the same losses; 0 if none
      `insurance received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you have already received for the same losses; 0 if none
      `likely payments counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars you are likely to receive for the same losses (not insurance or superannuation) that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `likely insurance counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you are likely to receive for the same losses that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `interim paid` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of interim assistance already paid to you on this application; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I be paid as a secondary victim` MEANS
    `s 14 -- the assistance a secondary victim can be paid`
        (`A secondary victim's expenses and losses` WITH
            `counselling` IS `counselling`
            `medical expenses` IS `medical`
            `loss of earnings in the first 2 years` IS `lost earnings`
            `other recovery expenses` IS `other recovery`)
        `exceptional`
        `maximum in force`
        `earnings maximum in force`
        (`What I have received or am likely to receive for the same losses` WITH
            `damages and compensation received, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `damages received`
            `insurance or superannuation payments received` IS `insurance received`
            `likely amounts the decision maker takes into account, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `likely payments counted`
            `likely insurance or superannuation payments the decision maker takes into account` IS `likely insurance counted`)
        `interim paid`

@export How much financial assistance can I be paid as a related victim (family, dependant or partner of a person killed) under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 16, 18, 37(3))? Up to $50 000 (indexed yearly) for counselling, medical expenses, distress, lost financial support for up to 2 years and other expenses from the death, less what you have received for the same losses (insurance and superannuation never reduce the distress amount) and any interim assistance.
GIVEN `counselling` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of reasonable counselling expenses you have incurred or are reasonably likely to incur
      `medical` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of medical expenses incurred, or reasonably likely, as a direct result of the death
      `distress` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars the scheme decision maker allows for the distress you experienced, or are likely to experience, as a direct result of the death
      `lost support` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars you would likely have received from the person who died in the 2 years after the death
      `death expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of other expenses incurred, or reasonably likely, as a direct result of the death (not funeral expenses, which are claimed separately)
      `other recovery` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of other expenses to help you recover from the death; counted only in exceptional circumstances
      `exceptional` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker accepts there are exceptional circumstances for the other recovery expenses
      `maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum for a related victim in force for this financial year under the indexation notice (s 72); enter 0 to use the Act's $50 000
      `damages received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of damages, compensation or other payments (but not insurance or superannuation) you have already received for the same losses; 0 if none
      `insurance received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you have already received for the same losses; 0 if none
      `likely payments counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars you are likely to receive for the same losses (not insurance or superannuation) that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `likely insurance counted` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of insurance or superannuation payments you are likely to receive for the same losses that the scheme decision maker decides to take into account; 0 if none
      `interim paid` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of interim assistance already paid to you on this application; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I be paid as a related victim` MEANS
    `s 16 -- the assistance a related victim can be paid`
        (`A related victim's expenses and losses` WITH
            `counselling` IS `counselling`
            `medical expenses` IS `medical`
            `distress` IS `distress`
            `money I would have received from them in the 2 years after the death` IS `lost support`
            `other expenses from the death` IS `death expenses`
            `other recovery expenses` IS `other recovery`)
        `exceptional`
        `maximum in force`
        (`What I have received or am likely to receive for the same losses` WITH
            `damages and compensation received, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `damages received`
            `insurance or superannuation payments received` IS `insurance received`
            `likely amounts the decision maker takes into account, other than insurance or superannuation` IS `likely payments counted`
            `likely insurance or superannuation payments the decision maker takes into account` IS `likely insurance counted`)
        `interim paid`

@export Can I be paid special financial assistance as a primary victim, and how much, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 9(5), 11)? It is paid on top of the other assistance, for a crime committed against you (or one you were exposed to as a child) in a category (A to D) the regulations prescribe: the category's minimum for a significant adverse effect, up to its maximum for an injury.
GIVEN `the offence` IS A `Kind of offence` @desc What the crime was, ignoring the offender's age, mental impairment or any defence: an offence punishable by imprisonment involving assault, injury or a threat of injury; a sexual offence (including rape); observation of the genital or anal region; stalking, child stealing or kidnapping; home invasion or aggravated home invasion; an offence the regulations prescribe; a conspiracy, incitement or attempt to commit one of these; or none of these
      `in Victoria` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime happened in Victoria
      `against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime was committed against you, or you were under 18 and were injured by hearing, seeing or being exposed to it
      `the harm` IS A `Kind of harm` @desc The harm you suffered: physical harm; psychological or psychiatric harm; a worsening of an injury you already had; trauma the scheme decision maker has determined to be an injury; trauma not so determined; grief or distress only; harm arising only from loss of or damage to property; or no harm
      `direct result` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your harm was a direct result of the crime
      `category` IS A `Category of act of violence` @desc The category the regulations put the crime in: category A, B, C or D, or not in a prescribed category
      `I committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you committed the crime and are criminally responsible for it
      `satisfied of adverse effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker is satisfied you suffered a significant adverse effect (grief, distress, trauma or injury) as a direct result
      `satisfied of injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker is satisfied you suffered an injury (physical, psychological or psychiatric harm) as a direct result
      `prescribed minimum` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum amount, in dollars, the regulations prescribe for the crime's category
      `prescribed maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount, in dollars, the regulations prescribe for the crime's category
GIVETH A `Special financial assistance`
`Can I be paid special financial assistance` MEANS
    `s 11 -- the special financial assistance I can be paid` `the offence` `in Victoria` `against me` `the harm` `direct result` `category` `I committed it` `satisfied of adverse effect` `satisfied of injury` `prescribed minimum` `prescribed maximum`

@export What is the maximum amount of victims of crime financial assistance in force this financial year, after indexation under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 72)? Each maximum ($60 000 and $50 000 overall, $20 000 for lost earnings) rises each year with the Melbourne consumer price index, rounded to the dollar, and never goes down.
GIVEN `last year's calculated amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount for last financial year as calculated by the formula, before any no-reduction rule (usually the same as last year's actual amount)
      `last year's actual amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount actually in force last financial year
      `CPI recent` IS A NUMBER @desc The all groups consumer price index for Melbourne for the last reference period (quarter) of the previous calendar year
      `CPI year earlier` IS A NUMBER @desc The same index for the corresponding period one year earlier
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the indexed maximum this year` MEANS
    `s 72 -- the amount in force this financial year` `last year's calculated amount` `last year's actual amount` `CPI recent` `CPI year earlier`

§§ `Applying (ss 22, 23, 24, 25, 27, 75)`

@export By when must I apply for financial assistance under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 23)? Usually within 3 years of the crime (or of the death, for a related victim or funeral costs); a child victim has until 3 years after turning 18; an adult victim of a sexual offence or family violence has 10 years; a child victim of child abuse or family violence may apply at any time. Late applications can still be decided if the scheme decision maker considers it appropriate (s 24).
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am applying as` @desc Who you are applying as: a primary victim; a secondary victim; a related victim; or the person who incurred the funeral expenses
      `day of the crime` IS A DATE @desc The day the crime happened; for a series of related crimes, the day of the last one
      `day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person killed by the crime died (used only for a related victim or funeral expenses; otherwise enter the day of the crime)
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved child abuse
      `family violence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved family violence
      `sexual offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved a sexual offence
      `counselling only, prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are applying only for counselling expenses in circumstances the regulations say allow applying at any time; answer no if unsure
GIVETH A `The time for applying`
`By when must I apply` MEANS
    `s 23 -- the time for applying` `who` `day of the crime` `day of the death` `my date of birth` `child abuse` `family violence` `sexual offence` `counselling only, prescribed`

@export If I apply for financial assistance on a given day, can my application be decided, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 23, 24)? An application in time is decided; a late one only if the scheme decision maker considers it appropriate.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am applying as` @desc Who you are applying as: a primary victim; a secondary victim; a related victim; or the person who incurred the funeral expenses
      `day of the crime` IS A DATE @desc The day the crime happened; for a series of related crimes, the day of the last one
      `day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person killed by the crime died (used only for a related victim or funeral expenses; otherwise enter the day of the crime)
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `child abuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved child abuse
      `family violence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved family violence
      `sexual offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime consisted of or involved a sexual offence
      `counselling only, prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are applying only for counselling expenses in circumstances the regulations say allow applying at any time; answer no if unsure
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) the application
      `decision maker considers it appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are late: whether the scheme decision maker considers it appropriate to decide your application anyway (having regard to your age, disability or illness, the offender's position of power or trust, the effects of the crime, homelessness, the length of any court case and other matters); answer no if not yet known
GIVETH A `Whether my application can be decided`
`Can my application be decided` MEANS
    `ss 23, 24 -- whether the application can be decided`
        (`s 23 -- the application is in time` (`s 23 -- the time for applying` `who` `day of the crime` `day of the death` `my date of birth` `child abuse` `family violence` `sexual offence` `counselling only, prescribed`) `day of the crime` `day I apply`)
        `decision maker considers it appropriate`

@export The crime happened before 18 November 2024: can I apply under Victoria's new Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 rather than the old Victims of Crime Assistance Act 1996 (s 75)? Not if you already got an award, or were refused one, under the old Act. The usual time limits still apply.
GIVEN `old award received` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you received an award (other than an interim award) under the Victims of Crime Assistance Act 1996 for this crime before 18 November 2024
      `old application refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you applied under the 1996 Act for this crime before 18 November 2024 and were refused
      `old application pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application under the 1996 Act for this crime was still undecided immediately before 18 November 2024
      `withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have withdrawn that undecided application
      `interim award made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an interim award was made on that undecided application
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply under the new scheme for an old crime` MEANS
    `s 75 -- I may apply under this Act for an act of violence before 18 November 2024` `old award received` `old application refused` `old application pending` `withdrawn` `interim award made`

@export Do I need to attach a statutory declaration to my application for financial assistance because the crime was not reported to police, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 22(1)(e), (2))? It sets out what happened and why you did not report it.
GIVEN `reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the crime has been reported to the police
      `special reporting category` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a special reporting category the regulations prescribe; answer no if unsure
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do I need a statutory declaration` MEANS
    `s 22(1)(e), (2) -- I must include a statutory declaration about not reporting` `reported` `special reporting category`

@export What is the most my lawyer or other helper can charge me for my victims of crime financial assistance application, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 25)? Only what the scheme decision maker allows; they cannot claim a lien on, or keep any costs out of, your assistance.
GIVEN `allowed` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars the scheme decision maker has allowed your lawyer or helper to charge you; 0 if nothing has been allowed
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most my representative can charge me` MEANS
    `s 25(2) -- the most my representative may charge me` `allowed`

@export I was sent a notice that my victims of crime financial assistance application will lapse unless I respond within 6 months: where does my application stand, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 27)? A lapsed application can be made again, subject to the time limits.
GIVEN `notice given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker has given you written notice that your application will lapse unless you respond within 6 months
      `day of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice was given to you
      `responded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have responded to the notice
      `day I responded` IS A DATE @desc The day you responded (if you have not, enter today)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where my application stands under s 27`
`Has my application lapsed` MEANS
    `s 27 -- where my application stands` `notice given` `day of the notice` `responded` `day I responded` `today`

§§ `The decision (ss 31, 36, 40)`

@export Must my application for victims of crime financial assistance be refused, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 31)? It must be if the crime was not reported to police in reasonable time or you did not help police (unless there were special circumstances or you are in a special reporting category), if you colluded with the offender, or if you already applied for the same crime. A no here does not mean you will be paid.
GIVEN `not reported in reasonable time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker is satisfied the crime was not reported to police within a reasonable time
      `did not assist police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker is satisfied you failed to give reasonable assistance to the police or others investigating the crime or arresting or prosecuting the offender
      `special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker considers there were special circumstances for the late report or the failure to assist
      `special reporting category` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in a special reporting category the regulations prescribe
      `collusion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the application is made in collusion with the person who committed (or is alleged to have committed) the crime
      `earlier application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied for assistance, in any capacity, for the same crime (decided or not)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my application be refused` MEANS
    `s 31 -- my application must be refused` `not reported in reasonable time` `did not assist police` `special circumstances` `special reporting category` `collusion` `earlier application`

@export Assistance was decided for me but has not all been paid: is it still payable, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 36(5))? Assistance not paid within 10 years after the decision stops being payable, unless it is held on trust for you.
GIVEN `day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision to pay you assistance was made
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `held on trust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the assistance is being held on trust for you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my unpaid assistance still payable` MEANS
    `s 36(5) -- the unpaid assistance is still payable` `day of the decision` `today` `held on trust`

@export Must I be given a victim recognition statement from the State, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 40)? If you are paid assistance as a victim and ask for one, the scheme decision maker must give you a statement acknowledging the effects of the crime and expressing the State's condolences. You may also ask for a recognition meeting (s 41), which is at the decision maker's discretion.
GIVEN `decided to pay me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the scheme decision maker has decided to pay you assistance
      `I am a victim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a primary, secondary or related victim (answer no if you are claiming only funeral expenses)
      `asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked for a victim recognition statement
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be given a recognition statement` MEANS
    `s 40 -- I must be given a victim recognition statement` `decided to pay me` `I am a victim` `asked`

§§ `Review, variation and repayment (ss 44 to 50, 74)`

-- This group arises only once a decision has been made on an application: ask "Has a decision been made on your
-- application?" before showing it.

@export Can I have a decision on my victims of crime financial assistance application reviewed, where, and by when, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 44 to 46)? A delegate's decision goes first to internal review by the scheme decision maker; the scheme decision maker's own decision, or an internal review decision, goes to VCAT; both within 28 days after you were given notice with reasons.
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc What the decision was: a refusal of your application (or a requirement to repay interim assistance); the amount of assistance; a refusal to vary your assistance; the amount on your application to vary; a requirement to repay assistance (or the amount); a decision about interim assistance; or another decision
      `who decided` IS A `Who made the decision` @desc Who made the decision, as the notice says: a delegate of the scheme decision maker; the scheme decision maker personally; or the scheme decision maker on an internal review
      `day of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the decision with its reasons
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For internal review: whether the scheme decision maker has extended the time to apply
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the extended time (if not extended, enter the day of the notice)
GIVETH A `Review open to me`
`Can I have the decision reviewed` MEANS
    `ss 44, 45, 46 -- the review open to me` `kind` `who decided` `day of the notice` `extended` `extended to`

@export If I apply for review of a victims of crime financial assistance decision on a given day, am I in time, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (ss 45, 46)? No answer means the decision is not reviewable.
GIVEN `kind` IS A `Kind of decision` @desc What the decision was: a refusal of your application (or a requirement to repay interim assistance); the amount of assistance; a refusal to vary your assistance; the amount on your application to vary; a requirement to repay assistance (or the amount); a decision about interim assistance; or another decision
      `who decided` IS A `Who made the decision` @desc Who made the decision, as the notice says: a delegate of the scheme decision maker; the scheme decision maker personally; or the scheme decision maker on an internal review
      `day of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the decision with its reasons
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For internal review: whether the scheme decision maker has extended the time to apply
      `extended to` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the extended time (if not extended, enter the day of the notice)
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or applied) for review
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my review application in time` MEANS
    `ss 45, 46 -- my application for review is in time` (`ss 44, 45, 46 -- the review open to me` `kind` `who decided` `day of the notice` `extended` `extended to`) `day of the notice` `day I apply`

@export By when can I apply to vary the financial assistance I was granted, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 48(2))? Within 10 years after the decision to pay, or until you turn 28 if you were under 18 at the decision, or a longer time the regulations prescribe.
GIVEN `day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the decision to pay you assistance was made
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `longer period prescribed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations prescribe a longer period for your kind of variation or circumstances; answer no if unsure
GIVETH A `The time for applying to vary`
`By when can I apply to vary my assistance` MEANS
    `s 48(2) -- the time for applying to vary` `day of the decision` `my date of birth` `longer period prescribed`

@export What is the most I can be required to repay of my victims of crime financial assistance, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 50)? If you later receive damages, compensation or other payments for the same losses, the lesser of the assistance and those payments; plus any decrease on review or variation; never more than the assistance paid.
GIVEN `assistance paid` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of assistance paid to you or for your benefit
      `later payments` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of damages, compensation or other payments you received after the assistance was paid, for the same losses, that were not taken into account but would have had to be; 0 if none
      `decrease` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars by which your assistance was decreased on review or variation; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most I can be required to repay` MEANS
    `s 50 -- the most I can be required to repay` `assistance paid` `later payments` `decrease`

@export I have an award from the old Victims of Crime Assistance Tribunal: can I apply to the scheme decision maker to vary it on a given day, under Victoria's Victims of Crime (Financial Assistance Scheme) Act 2022 (s 74)? Within 6 years after the award was first made, or until you turn 24 if you were under 18 then; a substantive variation only once.
GIVEN `day of the award` IS A DATE @desc The day the award was originally made under the Victims of Crime Assistance Act 1996 (not an interim award)
      `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or applied) for the variation
      `kind` IS A `Kind of variation` @desc The kind of variation: a minor variation, to give effect to the original intention of the award; or a substantive variation (any other)
      `already applied substantive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied under s 74 for a substantive variation of this award
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to vary my Tribunal award` MEANS
    `s 74 -- I may apply to vary my Tribunal award` `day of the award` `my date of birth` `day I apply` `kind` `already applied substantive`
